{
  "contract": "work-order",
  "about": "The work order (DOR-5063): computeWorkOrder({ items, projects, config, now }). Items and projects are written short and expanded by the test: an item is { id, p (priority 0-4), state (backlog | unstarted | started; default unstarted), size, project (a project id), labels, blockedBy, blocks, due (YYYY-MM-DD), created (ISO), triage, pin }; a project is { id, name, status, priority, target (null removes it; data only, it never moves a project or flags one), labels, blockedBy (project ids it waits on), created (ISO) }. 'base' is the shared fixture; the config is the default with order.personLabel set to owner/someone; each case may replace config.orderKeys or config.startedCap patch items (patch) and projects (projectPatch), and add projects (addProjects); a project may also give slug, and priority null removes it. expected.issues is the issue order, best first; expected.held, needsTriage, inFlight and personal list identifiers (held and needsTriage with their reason or missing fields); expected.inherits maps an item to the blocked item whose place it took; expected.banners is exact. expected.projects (DOR-5067) gives the now, next, later and personal lists as project ids in rank, the whole-list flags, each named project's flags and why text, and inherits (a project to the one waiting on it whose place it took). Each flip changes one field of one item on the base and names where that item lands (place, or 'unranked: <reason>' as rankOfIssue prints it). The test also shuffles every input and demands the same output.",
